t if  you fix the price of gold which is what  was done historically for periods of  great length both in the US and the UK  and elsewhere  it doesn't really ensure price stability  particularly over a short horizon  the relative price of gold and other  things is just too variable so pegging  the price of gold doesn't really give  you very good control over the general  price level which is normally what  you're interested in terms of creating  price stability it was also quite clear  historically that for example in the  United States it was a long term regular  history of bank runs and banking panics  which occurred under the gold standard  so the gold standard did not prevent  those kinds of disruptions in terms of  the financial and banking system so  question is whether we should think of  Milton is in some sense being a  Keynesian now the funny aspect of this  is that when Milton was writing  initially on monetary history and  similar events when he was writing in  the 1960s his views from were viewed as  anti Keynesian from a perspective of  typical macroeconomists and i think the  reason for that is that if you look at  Keynes's general theory then keynes  de-emphasized monetary disturbances as a  key element in business fluctuations and  he also didn't think that active  monetary policy was a an efficient  instrument for stabilizing the business  cycle  so therefore when when Milton was  particularly bringing out the important  real role of money in terms of economic  fluctuations as he did in a monetary  history that was counter to what Keynes  was arguing in the general theory and  for that reason Milton was viewed as not  being a Keynesian and with respect to  his 1960s thinking but later on typical  Keynesian sometimes called New Keynesian  'he's got the idea that by moving around  monetary policy in an active way you  could make use of the fact that money  was so powerful in influencing economic  activity and therefore use active  monetary policy to i