d price stability  because if you compare the period from  the  late-1980s up to say 2009 what was in  place earlier it looked like there was  great success in terms of having  achieved good inflation stability at a  low rate without compromising in terms  of economic growth I think that so that  looked like a major achievement not only  at the Federal Reserve but of central  banks worldwide so I thought that that  was a positive thing but things have  become much more complicated since the  Great Recession of 2008 2009 dr. Beryl  thank you very much my question has to  deal with the current participation rate  in the economy and what do you think  Milton Friedman's response would be to  the fact that it's the lowest since  World War two I think some of that has  to do with expanded transfer payment  programs which have diminished the  incentive of people to be in the  workforce and in general to supply labor  I'm not sure what Milton in particular  would say on this he'd certainly worry  about the disincentive effects related  to high transfer payments that were  available in the same way that he would  be worried about high tax rates so in  effect if you lose a lot of transfer  benefits by deciding to work and be in  the labor force  that's just like having a high marginal  tax rate on the income that you earn so  you'd want to kind of combine for an  effective tax rate what you have to pay  along with the benefits that you lose by  by working more and by being in the  labor force so I think Milton would have  said that this is a consequence of the  kind of fiscal policy that was followed  would respect the transfers but maybe  I'm putting words in Milton's mouth with  regard to what he would have said but  that that would be my vision of that  thank you very much for your speech  and I am wondering do you anticipate the  kind of financial or economic collapse  that is heralded on so many television  commercials including from dr. Stockman  and other luminaries I 