al instrument which is really what  it was doing by buying up particular  kinds of assets like that private sector  assets that that risks a lot of  interference or control by the Congress  because if the Fed is doing all kinds of  things beyond its mandate it becomes  more plausible that the Congress should  have 30 over them and they shouldn't  really be an independent entity but if  yes what do I think will happen if the  Congress gets more involved and they  audit the Fed and they start controlling  monetary policy that prism would be much  worse than the Fed acting on its own so  in that respect I'm not really a  supporter of the idea that Congress  shouldn't be more actively involved I  was more trying to advocate as I have to  people at the Fed over the last several  years that they shouldn't be doing the  kind of activities that compromise their  independence but I have to say again the  fact that inflation has been very low  and stable in this whole period is  something they can come back with and  say look in that respect these results  have been pretty good even though  economic growth has not been very strong  but I'm not really a in favor of  auditing the Fed and I don't know where  that's gonna go down the road in terms  of congressional monetary policy um dr.  Borrero earlier you spoke in my class  and you mentioned how you present your  Ricardian equivalence paper and when you  presented it Milton Friedman was in the  room along with George Stickle and Gary  Becker I believe it also just throughout  your time at Harvard in Chicago you've  been around a lot of other great  economists and I was kind of wondering  what it's like to work in that kind of  environment every single day  and also I'm an aspiring economist my  economist  it's always wondering if you have any  advice for someone like me so when I  went to Chicago in the early 1970s it's  true that the three great pillars of the  Chicago School were there Milton the  Longwood George Stigler and