ed to  recording equivalents and of course the  first place I looked was it at Adam  Smith's Wealth of Nations but I spent a  lot of effort and I couldn't find  anything before Ricardo that sounded  like Ricardian equivalence and if you  look at Adam Smith on these matters  about the government financing itself by  taxing versus borrowing it's actually  not very well organized and it doesn't  have a coherent argument about what the  effects would be so in the end I  accepted the idea that yes this is  recording equivalence and I think that  that's a very nice terminology so anyway  I of course greatly benefited by working  with Milton and Gary Becker and George  Stigler at Chicago looking back on it  now I probably shouldn't have left  Chicago in 1975 because even though the  place was basically crazy aside from  those three guys as you say working with  people like that sort of makes it all  worth it so maybe I should have stayed  there and possibly I would still be  there  if I had stayed there in 1975 but I  didn't end up doing it now despite what  Steve Forbes said last night Harvard  also has a lot of great economists I  think it's a terrific department and  even though Harvard has a reputation of  being kind of leftist which is  particularly well deserved if you look  outside of the Economics Department I've  found it a very congenial atmosphere for  me to operate in a lot of people have  different views from what I have on  macroeconomics  and about policy activism and that  what's happened particularly since the  financial crisis in 2009 but it's  certainly a climate where one can  operate would one's views without  feeling any kind of hostility or  anything like that  so I found Harvard a very good place to  operate in it it's very different from  the Chicago of the 1970s but I think  it's a very positive place I don't know  if that helps you in terms of advice for  you personally but that's my outlook on  particularly Chicago and Harvard we have  time for 