t if  you fix the price of gold which is what  was done historically for periods of  great length both in the US and the UK  and elsewhere  it doesn't really ensure price stability  particularly over a short horizon  the relative price of gold and other  things is just too variable so pegging  the price of gold doesn't really give  you very good control over the general  price level which is normally what  you're interested in terms of creating  price stability it was also quite clear  historically that for example in the  United States it was a long term regular  history of bank runs and banking panics  which occurred under the gold standard  so the gold standard did not prevent  those kinds of disruptions in terms of  the financial and banking system so  question is whether we should think of  Milton is in some sense being a  Keynesian now the funny aspect of this  is that when Milton was writing  initially on monetary history and  similar events when he was writing in  the 1960s his views from were viewed as  anti Keynesian from a perspective of  typical macroeconomists and i think the  reason for that is that if you look at  Keynes's general theory then keynes  de-emphasized monetary disturbances as a  key element in business fluctuations and  he also didn't think that active  monetary policy was a an efficient  instrument for stabilizing the business  cycle  so therefore when when Milton was  particularly bringing out the important  real role of money in terms of economic  fluctuations as he did in a monetary  history that was counter to what Keynes  was arguing in the general theory and  for that reason Milton was viewed as not  being a Keynesian and with respect to  his 1960s thinking but later on typical  Keynesian sometimes called New Keynesian  'he's got the idea that by moving around  monetary policy in an active way you  could make use of the fact that money  was so powerful in influencing economic  activity and therefore use active  monetary policy to it  economic stabilization so that  perspective became since the 1980s a  hallmark of Keynesianism and that  viewpoint fit very well with what milton  was arguing about money in the economy  in the 1960s so in that respect Milton's  thinking became what you might call us  too much of a Keynesian approach now  Milton also had a very important article  in the 1967 American Economic Review  which was his presidential address  called the role of monetary policy  this article along would work by Edmund  Phelps foreshadowed what Bob Lucas led  in the 1970s as the rational  expectations revolution in  macroeconomics so the major element  there  was that in the long run money would be  neutral and if you had big changes in  money in the long run it would just  affect price level and it wouldn't  affect real economic activity but in a  short run unanticipated monetary  movements and unanticipated movements in  prices could have major real economic  effects that was the idea of Lucas and  that was the material that was in  Milton's 1967 presidential address now  if you take that view point he still get  the idea that monetary policy can be  quite important in terms of stabilizing  the real economy and the long-run money  would be neutral but in the short-run  money would have major real consequences  and then it's not too much of a leap  from there to argue that a wise monetary  authority maybe even the Federal Reserve  could do good things in terms of  stabilizing the economy so Milton's view  of money then became a defense for  activism particularly in terms of how  the Federal Reserve would intervene and  it's no wonder that this part of  Milton's monetary macro framework was  then embraced by Keynesian in the 1980s  and then thereafter so the question is  how do you go from Milton's monetary  framework which seems to  suggests that monetary policy could do a  lot to stabilize the economy to Milton's  practical advice for monetary policy  which was not to be activists and t