aphs and I got this from  George's son Steven Stigler so Milton  here was getting a traffic ticket on the  Calumet expressway I think in 1972  that's outside of Chicago and he was  probably on the way to a tennis match so  I think here there's some lessons about  law and order because you can see that  Milton must have broken the law given  that he was pulled over but he also  seems to have a lot of respect for legal  authority since he's complying with the  instructions of the police officer so  gives you two important aspects about  the legal system one is that law and  order is very important for society but  secondly from an individual standpoint  sometimes it's rational to break the law  and that's apparently what happened in  this this case so I wanted to conclude  about the last time I actually saw  Milton so this was in the summer of 2006  at the Hoover Institution at Stanford  and I just chanced on Milton and Rose  walking on the steps outside of Hoover  and we got into some conversation and it  must have been on Milton's mind because  he told me out of the blue he said you  know I never planned to be living to age  94 and I don't think I've planned for it  properly and I'm not sure what I'm  supposed to be doing now that I'm still  alive  something like that so later that summer  I saw Milton for the last time lives in  San Francisco George Shultz is a fellow  of the Hoover Institution and of course  was Secretary of State and almost  everything else in the government was  hosting a party the party was in honor  of Tony Blair who was no longer in  office in the UK and I think Tony Blair  was looking for a job at the time was my  impression so there are many prominent  people present at this party that George  Shultz was hosting in San Francisco and  I recall asking someone I don't know why  I came up with this I said do you know  who is the most important person in this  room and the person I was talking to  didn't know how to respond to that and  kind of 