t certainly  not right over six years that monetary  expansion is a way to get higher really  Kodama growth so I think I'm agreeing  with the spirit of your question which  is that I don't think it's been  successful  dr. barrow thank you so much for your  time this evening my question is going  to go back to your slide under inflation  rates and where you basically talked  about central banks and how they can  adjust an inflation rates and therefore  improving economic situations and  well-being thereby  my question is what is the criteria used  by these banks in order to determine the  appropriate rate of adjustment given the  spirit of Friedrich Hayek's knowledge  problem as well if you could mind  talking about that sir thank you very  much I think many banks have used  versions of what now it's called a  Taylor rule where you look at in  particular the inflation rate and you  have in mind a kind of target inflation  rate typically around 2% per year and  then there's a kind of quantitative  adjustment about how much you move  nominal interest rates particularly at  the short-term and in response to the  inflation rate but it's somewhat of a  trial by error type procedure I don't  think that it's really something that  comes out of some fitted model that has  a lot of explanatory power it's also  true empirically that central banks  don't react just to inflation in terms  of moving around interest rates they  also react to some measures of real  economic activity and in particular the  Federal Reserve has tended to focus on  the labor market so it tends to adjust  interest rates in response to  unemployment and an expense in response  to weak employment growth those are the  things that they've moved in response to  I don't know that it's particularly  useful for the central bank to be having  that kind of response to the real  economy it doesn't seem like it was all  that harmful in terms of say 20 years of  experience where we seem to have  achieved pretty good price stability  because if you compare the period from  the  late-1980s up to say 2009 what was in  place earlier it looked like there was  great success in terms of having  achieved good inflation stability at a  low rate without compromising in terms  of economic growth I think that so that  looked like a major achievement not only  at the Federal Reserve but of central  banks worldwide so I thought that that  was a positive thing but things have  become much more complicated since the  Great Recession of 2008 2009 dr. Beryl  thank you very much my question has to  deal with the current participation rate  in the economy and what do you think  Milton Friedman's response would be to  the fact that it's the lowest since  World War two I think some of that has  to do with expanded transfer payment  programs which have diminished the  incentive of people to be in the  workforce and in general to supply labor  I'm not sure what Milton in particular  would say on this he'd certainly worry  about the disincentive effects related  to high transfer payments that were  available in the same way that he would  be worried about high tax rates so in  effect if you lose a lot of transfer  benefits by deciding to work and be in  the labor force  that's just like having a high marginal  tax rate on the income that you earn so  you'd want to kind of combine for an  effective tax rate what you have to pay  along with the benefits that you lose by  by working more and by being in the  labor force so I think Milton would have  said that this is a consequence of the  kind of fiscal policy that was followed  would respect the transfers but maybe  I'm putting words in Milton's mouth with  regard to what he would have said but  that that would be my vision of that  thank you very much for your speech  and I am wondering do you anticipate the  kind of financial or economic collapse  that is heralded on so many television  commercials including from dr. Stockman  and other luminaries I 